r  own workers the National Education  Association could do a great job of  turning out high school dropouts why  import more of them what can I get out  of Spawn do that  look actually let me respond to that  which is that you know protectionism  doesn't work when it is applied in trade  and protectionism doesn't work when it  is applied to labor I come from Detroit  where American automakers you know at a  at a time they were they were asked they  were great deal of protectionism against  Japanese car makers they had two  Japanese car makers like Toyota and  Honda had to agree to voluntarily sell  fewer cars in America because our  American companies couldn't compete what  was the option top up short of that the  upshot of that was that our car  companies became globally less  competitive we just couldn't make  products that were good enough to sell  abroad the same thing happens when you  protect labor one of the up shots of  letting low-skilled workers from Mexico  into the country is that has induced our  America our native-born American  children to acquire the skills to move  on to the higher rungs of the job ladder  so with the result there is a greater  incentive for them to actually finish  school because they can't simply count  on that plumbing job or that weed  pulling job and if you look at the  statistics and I would point you to a  very very good paper that Dan Griswold  of Cato Institute did which was  basically made the case as Mexican  workers have moved into the so-called  underclass in America the American  workers have actually moved into the  middle class and there is a very very  big correlation just to give you one  statistic from there between 1995 and  2004 the number of families living under  the official poverty level fell from 8  18.1 million to 7.6 million in America  immigrant household poverty rose by one  hundred and ninety four thousand but the  native households that were in poverty  decreased by six hundred and seventy  five thou